            "**`number`** — all numeric values, integers and decimals alike, as IEEE-754 double-precision floats. `42`, `3.14159`, `1e3` (1000), `-0`. Integers are exact up to 2⁵³−1 (`Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`); beyond that, precision is silently lost. Consequences you will meet:\n\n- **Floating-point arithmetic is approximate.** `0.1 + 0.2` is `0.30000000000000004`, not `0.3`, because 0.1 has no exact binary representation. Never compare floats for equality; compare with an epsilon (`Math.abs(a - b) < 1e-9`).\n- **Money must not be floats.** Store cedis and pesewas as integers (`GH₵24.50` → `2450`) or use a decimal library. A float total that drifts by a pesewa per thousand transactions is a real accounting failure.\n- **`NaN`** (Not-a-Number) is a *number*-type value meaning \"the result is undefined\": `0/0`, `\"abc\" * 2`, `parseInt(\"abc\")`. It is not equal to itself — `NaN === NaN` is `false` — so test with `Number.isNaN(x)`. `Infinity` and `-Infinity` come from division by zero.\n- **Falsy numbers**: `0`, `-0`, `NaN`. Everything else is truthy. Beware the legitimate falsy value: a valid ID of `0` or a price of `0` fails `if (price)` even though it is real.\n\n**`string`** — text, stored as UTF-16 code units. Immutable: every apparent modification produces a new string (`s.toUpperCase()` returns a new one; `s` is unchanged — a classic bug when you forget to assign it). Quote style is irrelevant; prefer `\"` or `'` for plain text and **backticks for templates**: `` `${name} has ${count} lessons` `` interpolates expressions and spans lines. Index with `s[0]`, length with `s.length` (code units, not characters — an emoji counts 2, from the encoding lesson). Core methods: `slice`, `substring`, `includes`, `startsWith`, `split`, `trim`, `replace`, `padStart`, `toLowerCase`.\n\n**`boolean`** — `true` or `false`. Produced by comparisons (`>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, `===`, `!==`, `==`), by logical operators, and by explicit conversion with `Boolean()`.\n\n**`undefined`** — the default: a declared-but-unassigned variable, a missing function argument, a missing object property, a function with no `return`. It means *\"nothing was provided\"*.\n\n**`null`** — the *intentional* absence of a value. The historical wart: `typeof null` is `\"object\"`, a backwards-compatibility bug from 1995 that can never be fixed. Test for it directly.\n\n**`symbol`** — a guaranteed-unique identifier, mostly used by frameworks as invisible property keys (that is how React's elements and iteration protocols work). You will consume symbols more than create them.\n\n**`bigint`** — integers beyond 2⁵³−1, written `123n`. Mixing with `number` throws rather than silently losing precision.\n\n**`function`** — in JavaScript, functions are values: they can be stored in variables, passed as arguments, returned from other functions. This is *the* fact that makes the language's style possible, and the functions lesson builds on it.\n\n**`object`** — everything else: `{}`, `[]`, `new Date()`, `/regex/`, a DOM element. Objects are mutable collections of key–value pairs; keys are strings (or symbols), values can be anything.\n\n**`typeof`** is the inspection tool: `typeof 42` → `\"number\"`, `typeof \"hi\"` → `\"string\"`, `typeof undefined` → `\"undefined\"`, `typeof {}` / `[]` / `null` → `\"object\"`, `typeof (() => {})` → `\"function\"`. Note the `null` lie and that arrays report `\"object\"` — use `Array.isArray(x)` for arrays and `x !== null && typeof x === \"object\"` for real objects.",
          code:
            "const price = 24.5;              // number (a float — never store money this way)\nconst pesewas = 2450;             // integer money: GH₵24.50\nconst title = \"Data Structures\";  // string\nconst isPaid = true;              // boolean\nlet middle;                       // undefined — declared, never assigned\nconst nothing = null;             // null — intentionally empty\n\nconst learner = { name: \"Amina\", level: 3 };  // object (reference)\nconst alsoLearner = learner;                   // second name for the SAME object\nalsoLearner.level = 4;\nconsole.log(learner.level);        // 4 — not 3! Both names point at one object.\n\nconst copy = { ...learner };       // a NEW object with the same fields\ncopy.level = 5;\nconsole.log(learner.level);        // 4 — untouched. Spread copied the top level.\n\nconsole.log(typeof middle);        // \"undefined\"\nconsole.log(typeof null);          // \"object\"  ← the historical lie\nconsole.log(Array.isArray([]));    // true — the correct array test",
