          body:
            "JavaScript has two equality operators, and choosing between them is a daily decision.\n\n**`===` (strict)** — true only if the values are the same *and* of the same type, with **no conversion**. This is what you want about 99% of the time.\n\n**`==` (loose)** — converts the operands to a common type first. Some of its results are surprising:\n\n```js\n0 == \"\"        // true   — both convert to 0/false-ish\n0 == false     // true   — both convert to 0\nnull == undefined  // true   — the ONLY loose equality that is safe to rely on\nnull === undefined // false\n\"1\" == 1       // true   — string converted to number\n[] == false    // true   — [] → \"\" → 0, false → 0\n[] == ![]      // true   — the famous paradox: ![] is false, [] → 0\nNaN == NaN     // false  — NaN is never equal to anything, even itself\n```\n\nThe rule: **use `===` and `!==`, always.** The one exception worth memorising is `x == null`, which is true for both `null` and `undefined` and is a concise \"is this absent?\" test. Note also the trap `value ?? fallback` versus `value || fallback`: `||` treats every falsy value as missing (so a legitimate `0` or `\"\"` gets replaced), while `??` only falls back on `null`/`undefined`. For counters, prices and user-typed strings, `??` is usually correct.\n\n**Coercion** also happens deliberately, and often usefully:\n\n- `Number(\"42\")` → `42`, `Number(\"42abc\")` → `NaN` (all-or-nothing), `parseInt(\"42abc\", 10)` → `42` (prefix parsing — always pass the radix).\n- `String(42)` → `\"42\"`, `` `${42}` `` → `\"42\"` — template interpolation always stringifies.\n- `Boolean(0)`, `Boolean(\"\")`, `Boolean(\"0\")`, `Boolean([])` → the first two are `false`; the last two are **`true`**. Non-empty and non-zero-ish strings and objects are truthy even when they look false.\n- In arithmetic, `+` with one string operand concatenates; `-`, `*`, `/` force numeric conversion: `\"5\" - 2` → `3`, but `\"5\" + 2` → `\"52\"`. This asymmetry is a classic bug when reading numbers from form inputs — which arrive as *strings*. Convert at the boundary: `Number(input.value)`.\n\nThe habit that prevents all of this: **normalise types at the edge of your program** (parse the input once, store the parsed value) and use strict comparisons inside.",
