          body:
            "- **`let` for everything.** `const` by default; `let` only when re-binding genuinely happens. A binding that never re-binds cannot be accidentally re-assigned.\n- **Thinking `const` freezes the object.** It freezes the binding, not the data. For deep immutability you need `Object.freeze` (shallow) or disciplined copying.\n- **`==` where you meant `===`.** The coercion table is a bug generator. `===`, except `x == null`.\n- **Comparing `NaN` to itself.** Use `Number.isNaN(x)`, not `x === NaN`.\n- **Money in floats.** Integer pesewas, or a decimal library. Never float.\n- **`\"0\"` or empty string from a form treated as a number.** Inputs return strings; `Number()` them and validate for `NaN` at the boundary.\n- **Forgetting that strings are immutable.** `name.toUpperCase()` alone does nothing; assign the result.\n- **Using `var`.** Function-scoped, hoisted, initialised as `undefined` until the line runs — a source of `undefined` bugs. `let`/`const` only.\n- **Accidental globals.** A missing `const` in one script overwrites a variable in another. Modules (ESM) prevent this; use them.\n- **Falsy checks on values that can legitimately be `0`.** `if (quantity)` fails for a real quantity of 0. Write `quantity != null` or `Number.isFinite(quantity)`.\n- **`typeof`-checking objects and forgetting `null`.** `typeof null === \"object\"`; guard explicitly.\n- **Trusting `parseInt` without a radix.** `parseInt(\"08\")` behaves differently across engines. Always `parseInt(s, 10)` — or better, `Number(s)`.\n- **Reassigning in a loop with `const`.** `for (const x of items)` is correct inside the loop (each iteration re-binds); `for (const i = 0; …)` throws. Declare the counter with `let`.",
